tpm-keyring-unlock (1.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.6.0. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * tpm-keyring-unlock-configure can give polkit prompts (App Center and
    snap installs, other admin actions) a fingerprint line of their own,
    which the lock screen never reads. It is asked the other way round:
    answer n at "Keep polkit prompts password-only?" to add the line,
    Enter keeps polkit password-only. With it, an enrolled finger alone
    approves root-level changes. tpm-keyring-unlock-deconfigure takes it
    back out.
  * Asked only where polkit prompts have no fingerprint and a finger is
    enrolled. To be asked after upgrading, run
    tpm-keyring-unlock-configure once more. Answer n at "Overwrite?" to
    keep the sealed secret.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:56:16 +0200

tpm-keyring-unlock (1.5.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.5.1. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * Fedora's gdm-fingerprint, which ships with no keyring line, is wired
    now: the configure step adds the keyring lines with the module between
    them. None of the added lines can vote, so the stack lets in exactly
    whom it let in before.
  * Under SELinux the configure step loads a one-rule policy module so
    that GDM's logins can reach the TPM. Removing this package unloads it.
  * After upgrading, run tpm-keyring-unlock-configure once more. It
    re-checks the PAM stacks an older version wired. Answer n at
    "Overwrite?" to keep the sealed secret.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:10:08 +0200

